  year = {1970},
  volume = {28},
  number = {4},
  pages = {511--514},
  doi = {10.2307/428491},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Truitt directly rebuts Baxandall's 1967 critique, defending Plekhanov's aesthetic theory against the charge of reductive economic determinism and arguing that Baxandall's reading mischaracterizes Plekhanov's actual, more historically nuanced account of the relation between social development and artistic form.

  RELEVANCE: Together with Baxandall's original article, this constitutes the one sustained pre-2000s English-language scholarly exchange specifically on the coherence of Plekhanov's aesthetics, giving the review a documented instance of the reductionism debate playing out in real time between named interlocutors.

  POSITION: Anti-reductionist rebuttal, prefiguring the more developed revisionist readings offered later by Sockol.
  },
  keywords = {plekhanov-aesthetics, vulgar-marxism, debate, Medium, INCOMPLETE}
}

@misc{sockol2011bridge,
  author = {Sockol, David},
  title = {A Bridge Between Kingdoms: The Marxist Aesthetics of Georgi Plekhanov},
  year = {2011},
  howpublished = {Thesis, Brandeis Institutional Repository},
  abstract = {Socialist Realism, the official artistic method of the Soviet Union, has remained a contentious but inadequately explored topic in historical scholarship. While its tenets that art must adhere to a realistic form and serve a didactic purpose have been well noted, its intellectual origins remain an open question. The arguments put forward by previous scholars regarding the genesis of Socialist Realism have largely focused on ahistorical analyses or conflated the similarities between it and previous aesthetic theories with causality, resulting in unsatisfactory conclusions. In my thesis, I argue that the ideas contained in Marxist theory concerning art and their development by the influential Russian theoretician Georgi Plekhanov must be considered among the intellectual foundation of Socialist Realism's doctrine. Beginning with an examination the writings of Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, I argue that a critical framework for judging and proscribing art was established in their theories of ideology and social development. Turning to Plekhanov's writings on art, I argue that he developed this framework into a stringent criticism or bourgeois and modern art alongside a staunch support of realistic and utilitarian art, thus strikingly predicting the tenets of Socialist Realism. Having illustrated that Plekhanov was able to construct a prescriptive aesthetic doctrine solely from Marxist ideas, I conclude that through his writings and widely noted influence over Soviet thought, Plekhanov's ideas form a hitherto unexplored avenue for investigating the origins of Socialist Realism that avoids the failings of previous inquiries and will prove beneficial to future research on this subject.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sockol argues that Plekhanov's writings on art, developed from a critical framework already present in Marx's and Engels's theories of ideology and social development, constructed a prescriptive aesthetic doctrine -- combining a stringent criticism of bourgeois and modern art with staunch support for realistic, utilitarian art -- that strikingly anticipated the later official tenets of Soviet Socialist Realism, and that this line of intellectual descent has been neglected relative to other proposed origins of Socialist Realism.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most sustained English-language treatment connecting Plekhanov's aesthetic theory forward to Socialist Realism, directly addressing this domain's question about Plekhanov's standing as founder of Marxist aesthetics and its downstream institutional consequences.

  POSITION: Traces continuity between Plekhanov's aesthetics and Socialist Realism, implying that Plekhanov's prescriptive, utilitarian conception of art bears responsibility for later doctrinal rigidity even though his own writings retain more argumentative nuance than the Stalin-era doctrine.
  },
  keywords = {plekhanov-aesthetics, socialist-realism, marxist-aesthetics, High}
}

@article{sockol2023verso,
  author = {Sockol, David},
  title = {Verso of the "Vulgar" Portrait: Georgi Plekhanov's Anti-Kantianism and Activist Aesthetic},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {484--501},
  doi = {10.1080/08935696.2023.2251333},
  abstract = {This essay examines Georgi Plekhanov, founder of the Russian Marxist movement and a supposed exemplar of "vulgar Marxism." It analyses Plekhanov's formulation of a Marxist aesthetic, interrogating the widespread image of Plekhanov's "vulgar" focus on economic determinism and his neglect of cultural forces' influence on revolutionary praxis, locating him instead within a fuller history of Marxist thought. A contextualization of Plekhanov's fin-de-siecle writings on art demonstrates that these texts were effectively a component of his contemporary polemics against critics of Marxist theory, and that within them he was pursuing his polemical agenda of criticizing Kantian philosophy and defending Marxist materialism. These dual aims manifested in Plekhanov's rejection of the Kantian notion of aesthetic disinterestedness and a stress on the formative role class conflict exerted on artistic creation, ultimately resulting in his formulation of an original aesthetic theory prescribing political commitment and thus mobilizing artworks towards revolutionary ends.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sockol argues that Plekhanov's aesthetic writings are best understood as fin-de-siecle polemical interventions against Kantian aesthetic disinterestedness and against critics of Marxism, rather than as naive "vulgar" economic reductionism; on this reading, Plekhanov's stress on class conflict as formative of artistic creation issues in a deliberately activist, tendentious aesthetic theory aimed at mobilizing art for revolutionary ends, not a mechanical reflection theory.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most recent and most sustained defense of Plekhanov's aesthetic coherence against the standard "vulgar Marxism" charge, directly answering this domain's question of whether Plekhanov's theory reduces art to sociological determinants without remainder, and situating his anti-Kantianism as the philosophical core of his rejection of "art for art's sake."

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Plekhanov against the "vulgar Marxist" label, reframing his aesthetics as polemically anti-Kantian and activist rather than reductive.
  },
  keywords = {plekhanov-aesthetics, anti-kantianism, vulgar-marxism, tendentious-art, High}
}

@article{nascimento2014plekhanov,
  author = {Nascimento, Rodrigo Alves do},
  title = {Plekhánov e a crítica estética marxista: entre a intenção e o gesto},
  journal = {RUS (São Paulo)},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {3},
  number = {3},
  pages = {85--94},
  doi = {10.11606/issn.2317-4765.rus.2014.88704},
  abstract = {If one wants to understand the history of Marxism in Russia, Georgi Plekhanov is an important and underrated thinker. Besides the introduction of classical texts by Marx in that country, Plekhanov was also responsible for the political guidelines that lead the organized workers' movement up until 1914 and the author of several innovative essays on Marxism and aesthetics. This paper will discuss this last aspect of Plekhanov's work, with emphasis to his essay "Art and Social Life", from 1912. I will discuss Plekhanov's formulations and the contradictions of a restrictive method, which did not prevent him from making important contributions to the understanding of the specificities of the aesthetic realm. In a dialogue with the bielinskian tradition, Plekhanov explored and started the debates of a Marxist aesthetic theory, an area of Marxism then unexplored.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nascimento reconstructs Plekhanov's aesthetic criticism, focused on "Art and Social Life" (1912), showing how Plekhanov's method -- for all the contradictions of what the author calls a "restrictive method" -- nonetheless made genuine contributions to understanding the specificity of the aesthetic realm, and situates this within Plekhanov's dialogue with Belinsky's earlier Russian critical tradition.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an independent, non-Anglophone reconstruction of the same coherence question (restrictive/reductive method versus genuine aesthetic insight) at stake in the Baxandall/Truitt and Sockol material, and explicitly credits Plekhanov with founding Marxist aesthetic theory as a then-unexplored area of Marxism.

  POSITION: Balanced/critical-sympathetic reading: acknowledges Plekhanov's method as restrictive while crediting him as founder of Marxist aesthetic debate.
  },
  keywords = {plekhanov-aesthetics, marxist-literary-criticism, art-social-life, High}
}

@book{dellavolpe1978critique,
  author = {Della Volpe, Galvano},
  title = {Critique of Taste},
  year = {1978},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Della Volpe develops a rival materialist aesthetics that seeks to rehabilitate the rational and intellectual nature of art against both formalist irrationalism (Croce, New Criticism) and what he identifies as the "sociological reductionism" of Plekhanov and Lukacs, arguing that conceptual meaning is always inseparable from aesthetic effect rather than epiphenomenal to social/class position.

  RELEVANCE: Represents an internal Marxist critique of Plekhanov's aesthetics from within twentieth-century Italian Marxism, giving the review a documented instance of Plekhanov's "sociological reductionism" being named and rejected by a later, methodologically sophisticated Marxist aesthetician rather than only by non-Marxist critics.

  POSITION: Rival Marxist aesthetic tradition explicitly defined in opposition to Plekhanov's (and Lukacs's) sociological method.
  },
  keywords = {marxist-aesthetics, sociological-reductionism, della-volpe, Medium, INCOMPLETE}
}

@article{luyan2015lefebvre,
  author = {Yan, Lu},
  title = {An Interpretation of Henri Lefebvre's Introduction to Aesthetics},
  journal = {The Journal of Aesthetic Education},
  year = {2015},
  abstract = {Ignored by Chinese and international academic circles, Henri Lefebvre's Introduction to Aesthetics was not only a representative work in Marxist aestheticism on its publication in the late 1940s, but also contravened socialist realism, the dominant ideology of the Marxist camp at that time. In his review of the history of aesthetics he criticized Kant and Hegel and eulogized Plato and Diderot, showing his theoretic bent to apply philosophical aesthetics to daily life. Lefebvre lauded Marx's Manuscripts of Paris and the idea of all-round development put forward in this book was to become the immediate inspiration for his theory of the "whole person." His argument with Plekhanov on the origin and functions of art is rather their interpretations of the relationship between art and social life from the perspectives of the material and spiritual aspects of culture respectively.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lu Yan reconstructs Henri Lefebvre's "Introduction to Aesthetics" and its dispute with Plekhanov over the origin and functions of art, characterizing the disagreement as one between Plekhanov's emphasis on the material/social aspects of culture and Lefebvre's emphasis on its spiritual dimension.

  RELEVANCE: Documents a direct instance of a later Western Marxist aesthetician (Lefebvre) explicitly arguing against Plekhanov's account of the relationship between art and social life, showing the theory's afterlife as a foil for subsequent Marxist aesthetics distinct from the Della Volpe/Lukacs lineage.

  POSITION: Reports a rival Marxist-humanist critique (Lefebvre) of Plekhanov's materialist derivation of art from social life.
  },
  keywords = {plekhanov-aesthetics, lefebvre, marxist-aesthetics-reception, Medium}
}

@article{toma2025politicization,
  author = {Toma, Vlad-Florin},
  title = {An Introduction on the Politicization of Arts in Imperial Russian Left-Wing Movements: From Western Models Towards Original Developments},
  journal = {Euro-Atlantic Studies},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {7},
  number = {2024},
  pages = {7--32},
  doi = {10.31178/eas.2024.7.1},
  abstract = {Although the politicization of artistic content has been around us since Antiquity, a much more refined approach towards this segment came to shape during the nineteenth century, as philosophical, doctrinary and ideological thought became much more complex. Across Europe, during these times of ideological strifes, art has started to regain attention, but this time in political circles, on all sides of the political specter, as a tool to further and enhance various political agendas. Usually overlooked by historians in favour of studying more contemporary phenomenons such as Soviet-era Socialist Realism head-on, the basis for a politicised direction in arts by the left-wing movements in Russia was established much earlier than the postrevolutionary period. Due to a lack of first-hand articles on this certain aspect, this paper will be centered around the idea of a disseminator of general considerations, that is based upon text analysis of different fundamental political or philosophical works that hold such information regarding the evolution towards political aesthetics in the left-wing intelligentsia or political discourse in the Russian Empire. Given the fact that this concept is still underresearched, the main goal of this study is to give a framework to the main aspects of the prerevolutionary beginnings of political aesthetics or discourse on art in the aforementioned leftist movements. A secondary goal of this research is to create an efficient introduction resource for students or other interested readers. The paper is structured as follows: The beginnings of such processes from Western models to the Russian case, being represented by the contribution of Nikolay Gavrilovich Chernyshevsky, a view upon Classical Marxist approaches towards art and aesthetics, the birth of Russian Orthodox Marxist theory, starting with the works of Gerogi Valentinovich Plekhanov and ending with the political praxis of Plekhanov's ideas, represented by the actions of Vladimir Ilych Ulianov (Lenin).},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Toma traces the prerevolutionary genealogy of politicized art and aesthetic discourse in the Russian left-wing intelligentsia, arguing that the basis for a politicized direction in the arts predates Soviet-era Socialist Realism and runs from Chernyshevsky's utilitarian aesthetics through Plekhanov's founding of "Russian Orthodox Marxist" aesthetic theory to its political practice under Lenin.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Plekhanov's aesthetics within a longer indigenous Russian tradition of politicized/utilitarian art criticism (Chernyshevsky) rather than treating it as a purely derivative application of Western Marxism, and traces the line from Plekhanov's theory to Bolshevik political practice.

  POSITION: Historical-genealogical account placing Plekhanov as the pivotal founder of Russian Marxist aesthetic-political theory between Chernyshevsky's utilitarianism and Leninist practice.
  },
  keywords = {plekhanov-aesthetics, chernyshevsky, politicized-art, marxist-literary-criticism, Medium}
}

